<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Marshall</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1755">1755</a>–<a href="/years/1835">1835</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Marshall was the fourth chief justice of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, serving from 1801 to 1835, the longest tenure in the court's history. In decisions such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marbury-v-madison">Marbury v. Madison</a> (1803), which established judicial review, and McCulloch v. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> (1819), he strengthened the Supreme Court and the federal government; he had earlier served as secretary of state under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-adams">John Adams</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-marshall.jpg" alt="John Marshall" width="320" height="397" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henry Inman · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Marshall_by_Henry_Inman,_1832.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Cherokee Nation v. Georgia in 1831 Chief Justice <b>John Marshall</b> described Native nations as “domestic dependent nations”, and in Worcester v. Georgia, decided on 3 March 1832, the Supreme Court held that Georgia’s laws had no force in Cherokee territory.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cherokee-nation">Cherokee Nation · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In Marbury v. Madison in 1803 the Supreme Court under Chief Justice <b>John Marshall</b> asserted the power of judicial review.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-states">United States · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Chief Justice <b>John Marshall</b> ruled that the Supreme Court could strike down acts of Congress that conflicted with the Constitution.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-24">24 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Chief Justice <b>John Marshall</b>'s strict reading of treason doomed Jefferson's prosecution.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-1">1 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Germantown, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.61,-77.74&amp;year=1807" aria-label="Germantown on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2620 778 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of John Marshall"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3070 1048h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3070 1048h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.61,-77.74&amp;year=1807">What was here in 1807? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It is emphatically the province and duty of the Judicial Department to say what the law is.&#8221;</p><footer>Opinion of the Court, Marbury v. Madison (1803) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Marbury_v._Madison/Opinion_of_the_Court" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The very essence of civil liberty certainly consists in the right of every individual to claim the protection of the laws whenever he receives an injury. One of the first duties of government is to afford that protection.&#8221;</p><footer>Opinion of the Court, Marbury v. Madison (1803) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Marbury_v._Madison/Opinion_of_the_Court" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;In considering this question, then, we must never forget that it is a Constitution we are expounding.&#8221;</p><footer>Opinion of the Court, McCulloch v. Maryland (1819) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/McCulloch_v._Maryland/Opinion_of_the_Court" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The Government of the United States has been emphatically termed a government of laws, and not of men. It will certainly cease to deserve this high appellation if the laws furnish no remedy for the violation of a vested legal right.&#8221;</p><footer>Marbury v. Madison (1803), opinion of the Supreme Court &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Marbury_v._Madison/Opinion_of_the_Court" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;That the power to tax involves the power to destroy; that the power to destroy may defeat and render useless the power to create…&#8221;</p><footer>Opinion of the Court, McCulloch v. Maryland (1819) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/McCulloch_v._Maryland/Opinion_of_the_Court" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Let the end be legitimate, let it be within the scope of the Constitution, and all means which are appropriate, which are plainly adapted to that end, which are not prohibited, but consist with the letter and spirit of the Constitution, are Constitutional.&#8221;</p><footer>Opinion of the Court, McCulloch v. Maryland (1819) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/McCulloch_v._Maryland/Opinion_of_the_Court" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
